A closure is a function together with references to the surrounding lexical environment in which it was created. Returning an inner function does not copy a local value into the function; it preserves access to the binding, so later calls can observe or change that binding. This enables factories, private state, memoization, and callbacks. The retained environment also has a cost: a long-lived callback can keep referenced objects reachable, so remove listeners or release references when their lifetime ends.

```ts caption="The returned function closes over the mutable binding."
function counter() {
  let n = 0;
  return () => ++n;
}
const next = counter();
next(); // 1
```

A `var` loop variable is function-scoped, so callbacks created by the loop share one binding. If callbacks run after the loop, they all read its final value. A `let` declaration in a loop creates a per-iteration binding, so each callback gets the corresponding index. A function factory or an explicitly passed argument provides the same separation. The key distinction is that closures capture bindings, not snapshots of the binding's value.

```ts caption="Block-scoped iteration bindings avoid the shared `var` binding."
const fns: (() => number)[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) fns.push(() => i);
fns.map((fn) => fn()); // [0, 1, 2]
```

The order is `A`, `D`, `B`, `C`. The script's synchronous statements run first, so `A` and `D` are logged before any callback. The promise reaction is queued as a microtask; the timer callback is queued as a task. After the current job finishes, the microtask queue is drained before the event loop takes the timer task. The `0` delay does not make the timer synchronous or guarantee a precise execution time.

```ts caption="Output: A, D, B, C in browser-style host scheduling."
console.log('A');
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('B'));
setTimeout(() => console.log('C'), 0);
console.log('D');
```

Promises can represent concurrent work, but `Promise.all` over an unbounded collection can overload a service, connection pool, or browser. Use a worker pool or semaphore: keep at most a chosen number of jobs in flight, start another when one settles, and define how failures are reported. Preserve input order only if the API requires it; otherwise stream results. If jobs have side effects, add idempotency and cancellation. Measure the limit rather than assuming the largest possible parallelism is fastest.

```ts caption="A worker pool bounds the number of active operations."
async function mapLimit<T, R>(items: T[], limit: number, fn: (x: T) => Promise<R>) {
  const out: R[] = []; let next = 0;
  async function worker() { while (next < items.length) { const i = next++; out[i] = await fn(items[i]); } }
  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker));
  return out;
}
```
